PB,

Just like the other versions of Windows since 95, the NT 4.0 drivers for the
Alpha require an EXACT match of chipset.  A close match is not good enough, and
the drivers enforce the exact match.

Back in the bad old days of Windows 3.1, it was possible to install some fairly
basic drivers and run all manner of video cards.  For instance, when I was
testing Compaq surplus Matrox cards by the hundreds, I loaded up the basic
Millenium driver on a Windows 3.1 hard disk, and I was able to test basic
functions of the Millenium 2 PCI, G100 AGP, and G200 AGP without making one
change in the software itself.  The later chips are all upward compatible with
the previous ones.

Either you'll need to find Millenium 2 drivers for NT 4.0 Alpha, you'll need to
install a graphics card with exact Alpha drivers.  If neither the Matrox nor the
DEC/Compaq/HP web site does not have NT 4.0 Millenium 2 drivers, best look for
another graphics card.  Pity, too, because the Millenium 2 is faster than the
original Millenium... Ben Myers

