I run Ultrix, NetBSD, and good ol' 4.3tahoe on various VAXen. each has
its appeal. NetBSD is modern , has source, and supports most anything
one might ever want to run, but supports less hardware and X is very
spottily covered on vax. Ultrix supports the hardware well, has DECnet,
and a great X server.. but it's older, technically has legal license
issues which even if theyll never bother might stop some people, and its
hard to get some newer stuff compiled in ultrix.. and 4.3 is tiny, fast,
has okay support, has source, and is a total retro trip to the 80s.. but
its a _lot_ of work getting modern stuff compiled under it.
try them all, and see which one suits you. personally, i think the all-round
best for someone who doesnt need Xwindows is NetBSD of the 1.3.2-1.4.2 era,
the 1.5's get a bit more bloat (still quite usable, it is not anywhere
near the amount of bloat in linux, and even while bloated, NetBSD is still
implemented _well_) (i dont really dig netbsd 1.6 though), and are modern
enough to support much anything youd ever want to run on a vax.
4.3 is a speed demon..
(i hacked 4.3tahoe to work on a vax4000/600... wow it is fast!)

happy hacking,
isildur
www.vaxpower.org/~isildur/computers.html to see my computer menagerie


