Bob,

The best suggestion I can make is to download Western Digital's hard drive
diagnostics from its web site, and run.  The AC12100 is a fairly old model, so
it may have bitten the dust.  If the drive electronics are bad and the drive
heads and platters are still good, you can replace the small circuit board on
the drive.  With WD (and most others, I presume), the model of the drive must be
an EXACT match.  I have been lucky enough to find an exact match for one of my
clients in dire need of the data on the drive.

But first, run the diagnostics and see what happens.  Let me know.  I may have a
working AC12100 of the same model which you could buy and cannibalize to get the
data from the drive.  Do so only on the system where the drive was installed
originally though.  BIOSes among various models of motherboards do have
variations in mapping hard drive geometry, which complicates the process of
switching a drive to another system.

One more thing.  The PC5510 is old enough that replacing the C2032 3v lithium
CMOS battery may be a magic cure for the symptoms you are seeing.  When the
motherboard CMOS fails to hold its data, all manner of problems can ensue.

... Ben Myers

