it wasn't the first attempt at a technology, but it was definitely one
of the grandest (and boldest, given the speeds that thing runs at) uses
for it. Not CMOS, but ECL. ECL has been around since the late 60s,
and haD the properties of switching faster than CMOS and also using
tons more power. Doing an ECL machine that big running at frequencies
that high and fitting it in a box as small as they could without
water cooling _was_ quite a feat. The multichip modules were very
cutting edge for their time, and the thermal management was quite advanced,
to get 30 KW out of that cabinet entirely by air cooling..
In the long run, though, they should have realized that the market for
million-plus dollar machines was too thin to justify such a huge project..
i think they had taken on too much of the IBM mentality after moving so
much into IBM's traditional markets..
and not 2 years later the NVAX came around, and in terms of price/performance
completely evaporated whatever advantage a 9000 had. If only DEC had
taken the couple billion they lost on the vax 9000 and instead sunk it
into NVAX machines! higher volume, lower cost, larger market penetration,
etc etc etc..

sitting next to my vax 4000/600,
Isildur



