 Well, for a few bits NetBSD is ahead and for others Linux is.  Actually
the I/O ASIC SCSI works well for many years now -- it was only the PMAZ-A
that used to fail and then only for the R4k DECstations -- due to the lack
of an adequate generic R4k iomem coherency primitive.  The reason was
probably the PCI-centric design of Linux -- most testers run Linux on
computers equipped with the PCI bus and the bus has its own coherency
primitives that hide the bug. 

 Give us more skilled people with appropriate hardware and such flaws
won't happen anymore. ;-) 

> Seriously, NetBSD is far and away the best-choice Unix for these
> old systems. 

 I dare not do a system comparison, but these days Linux is an adequate OS
for these computers for many services as well.  See
http://www.linux-mips.org/ for a resource list. 

