Jerome Fine replies:

The news groups alt.sys.pdp11 and vmsnet.pdp-11 are more
usual for this topic, but I do monitor both of the other groups
as does Megan Gentry - obviously.

> I support (on PC's) a legacy disk format based on the RT-11 file system,
> as realized on RX01 8" floppies but ported to 3.5" 720K / 1.44M.  I'm
> not sure, but this may be known as RX50.

As far as I know, RX50 are SSDD 5 1/4" floppy media that have
80 tracks per inch and 10 sectors per track.  That is quite different
from the normal PC DSDD 5 1/4" media which use 40 tracks per
inch and 9 sectors per track.  Also the total capacity of the RX50
is 800 sectors of 512 bytes per sector.  I have NO idea what
interleave is used, but on the PDP-11 with an RQDX1,2,3, I
presume the controller takes care of the problem.

As far as I know, RX23 are identical to the 3 1/2" 1.44 MByte floppy
media on a PC (and the same drives are often used) with a format
of 16 sectors per track and possibly 96 tracks per inch.  The total
capacity of both the RX23 and the 1.44 MByte PC HD floppy
media are 2800 sectors of 512 bytes each.  Note that the RX23
is NOT a standard media for the PDP-11, but does seem to be
supported via the controller or host adapter if the correct number
of blocks (sectors) is provided in a size request to the controller
or host adapter made by the PDP-11 operating system.

> This is "Y2K" for the RT-11 date format... it's based at 1972 and uses 5
> bits for year. 1972 + 31 = 2003.  So what happens next year?`

Just keep on trucking, as Megan Gentry has already replied ...

> The actual layout of an RT11 date word is 0mmmmmdddddyyyyy.  That unused
> 0 bit is rather tempting as a year-extender, but before I go off on my
> own, I wondered if there's been any discussion of this and if so, what
> was decided?

The month field was never actually 5 bits, so those extra two
bits at the top were taken over.  As Megan Gentry stated, the date
range under V5.06 (ONLY the utilities MACRO and BUP were
completed and even the monitors were not Y2K compliant) is
1972 up to 2099.

> Along these lines, is there an online resource anywhere that documents
> the internals of the various physical floppy formats used by DEC, and
> the application of RT-11 to those formats?  In particular, I'm
> interested in the sector interleaving used on the two common 3.5"
> flavors (720K and 1.44M).

I have no idea at all about how the sector interleave is done since
that seems to be a controller or host adapter function as far as
PDP-11 operating systems are concerned.  Most likely there
is some interleaving done, but it is probably the same as for the
PC systems under DOS/Windows.  However, you can probably ...

> Thanks in advance for any tips, insights, etc.
>      Jim Mack
>      Editing Services Co

look in the source code for the program PUTR.COM which is
a DOS/WINDOWS 95/98 program available at:
http://www.dbit.com/pub/putr/

If there is an interleave used which is different from the
standard PC interleave, then PUTR would need to be able
to handle that.  PUTR handles RX50, RX33, RX23 and
probably RX24 which might be those 720 KBytes 3 1/2"
floppy media.

NOTE:  PUTR handles the RT-11 file structure as a matter
of course, being able to switch between DOS/WINDOWS 95/98
and RT-11 file structures.

On the same site, there is also the Erstaz-11 hobby emulator
and information about the commercial versions as well.  I realize
that you don't use RT-11 in general.  Also, this question seems
related to commercial activity, so if you wish/want/need a Y2K
version of RT-11 immediately, that is $ US 1600 from Mentec
just for the V5.07 distribution and DOCs.  License is extra.

My goal is to produce a hobby Y2K/Y10K V5.03 hobby version
before the end of this year starting with RT11FB and RT11XM.
I will backward port the Y2K code from V5.04G that was done
over 5 years ago - for a commercial customer.  However, due to
the almost total lack of interest by everyone, the Y2K/Y10K V5.03
changes will probably be available only to hobby users - and very
few hobby users have indicated any interest.

If you have any other questions, please ask.  Let us know what
happens with your project.  GOOD  LUCK and Happy New Year.

Sincerely yours,

Jerome Fine
