> As far as I know, RX50 are SSDD 5 1/4" floppy media that have
> 80 tracks per inch and 10 sectors per track.  That is quite different
> from the normal PC DSDD 5 1/4" media which use 40 tracks per
> inch and 9 sectors per track.  Also the total capacity of the RX50
> is 800 sectors of 512 bytes per sector.  I have NO idea what
> interleave is used, but on the PDP-11 with an RQDX1,2,3, I
> presume the controller takes care of the problem.

Ah, now I remember.  RX50 was the format used on a different system for
its 5.25" floppies.  I've written handlers for all of these types, but
it's been >10 years since I had to look at them (and no source any
more).  These were a bear, as I recall, because of the bizarre
interleave and skew used.


> As far as I know, RX23 are identical to the 3 1/2" 1.44 MByte floppy
> media on a PC (and the same drives are often used) with a format
> of 16 sectors per track and possibly 96 tracks per inch.  The total
> capacity of both the RX23 and the 1.44 MByte PC HD floppy
> media are 2800 sectors of 512 bytes each.  Note that the RX23
> is NOT a standard media for the PDP-11, but does seem to be
> supported via the controller or host adapter if the correct number
> of blocks (sectors) is provided in a size request to the controller
> or host adapter made by the PDP-11 operating system.

I've never come across the name RX23, but I'm sure you're right.

The situation is this: two competing manufacturers using LSI-11 minis,
both of whom used RX01 drives with compatible SSSD 8" media, which
eventually became impossible to support for lack of disks and drives.

Both added 3.5" drives, but they did it in incompatible ways.  The first
decided to treat the 3.5" as though it were a miniature RX01 disk,
mapping 484 blocks onto the 80x9x512 format, and keeping all the gory
soft-interleave and skew, with adaptations for the larger sectors.  This
is one format I have to support, and it's plain now that it's NOT a
supported DEC format.

The second apparently more closely followed a DEC convention (RX24?),
using 1:1 interleave (maybe 2:1) and no skew.  This is much more sane
and easy to support.


