The 5.25inch HD drive format is very similar to the 8 inch format,
except that it allows 80 tracks instead of 77, and doesn't require
the Reduced Write Current signal on inner cylinders.  Instead, they
use the RWC line to allow writing to DD disks, which naturally
require lower write current.  So, when using such a drive do NOT
connect the RWC line.  (I think it should be grounded, but I forget
now.)

The 3.5inch HD drive is similar to the 5.25inch HD drive, except
that it runs at 300RPM (like 5.25in DD and 3.5inch DD) instead of
360RPM.   You can get a few more sectors per track, but it works
fine without them.

